//? Title: Learned Image Codec: Counting the Complete File
//? Subtitle: Frozen autoencoder, fitting-only scalar quantization and a fresh packet decoder
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
This compact learned-transform codec sends an actual self-contained file: byte symbols,
fitting ranges, decoder parameters and source identities. A visible prerequisite trains the
original 784–32–784 autoencoder for three epochs and 96 Adam updates. The codec itself adds
zero updates. On 200 fixed held-out MNIST images, the symbol payload is 6,400 bytes, but the
complete file is 110,972 bytes, including 103,488 decoder bytes. The actual original-image PNG
collection is smaller at 49,889 bytes. Quantization changes test pixel MSE from .050325811 to
.050327014; the underlying blurry reconstructions remain. A fresh process can decode using
only the file, without the encoder, images or fitting data. Every byte category and all ten
fixed unfiltered cases are retained.
)
\toc

> Analysis, quantization, transmission and synthesis

The encoder maps each 784-pixel image to 32 nonnegative ReLU coordinates. Per-coordinate
minima and maxima are fitted to 1,000 fitting codes. Each test code then becomes 32 uniform
8-bit symbols. The receiver reconstructs code values from the stored bounds and applies the
stored sigmoid decoder. Encoder weights are not needed at the receiver. Decoder weights,
quantizer bounds and source identities are transmitted in this experiment's full-file rate.

Learned image compression can jointly optimize nonlinear transforms and quantization for
rate-distortion performance, as in [Ballé, Laparra and Simoncelli (ICLR 2017)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01704).
Here the original small MSE autoencoder is frozen before post-training quantization. There is
no entropy model, entropy coding or rate-distortion objective in this local teaching format.

