> Code error reaches the decoder through its own sensitivity

A one-coordinate decoder sigmoid(4z-2) shows how a small code displacement affects a
probability. It is not a second encoder and needs no input image. The error bound of half
a quantizer step applies to coordinates inside a nonconstant fitted interval; clipped queries
can have larger code error. It does not by itself bound pixel error independently of decoder
weights and activation slopes. The actual experiment measures both reconstruction errors.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/codec_toy_decoder.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  namespace m  = n::models;
  const auto x = n::linspace(0, 1, 101).reshape({101, 1}), w = n::full({1, 1}, 4.),
             bias      = n::full({1}, -2.);
  const auto exact     = m::LearnedCodecDecode(x, w, bias);
  const auto inverse   = m::DequantizeCodec(m::QuantizeCodec(x, {{0.f}, {1.f}}), {{0.f}, {1.f}});
  const auto quantized = m::LearnedCodecDecode(inverse, w, bias);
  const auto error     = n::abs(quantized - exact);
  std::print("Maximum toy probability change: {:.9f}.\n", n::max(error).item());
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.lines(
          x.to_vector<double>(), error.to_vector<double>(), "Probability error", {220, 38, 38}, 2.)
      .y_limits(0, .0021)
      .y_ticks({0, .0005, .001, .0015, .002}, {"0", "0.0005", "0.0010", "0.0015", "0.0020"})
      .x_label("Original code z")
      .y_label("Absolute probability change")
      .title("A byte step is transformed by the explicit decoder")
      .save_svg("build/codec_toy_decoder.svg");
}
```
