\image(figures/codec_all_case_errors.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All 200 cases remain in source order; nearly coincident series show that decoder reconstruction quality dominates here.)
\alttext(Per-case errors fluctuate across the entire held-out pool, with packet and unquantized errors nearly coincident.)

> Fidelity, validation and limits

The canonical API owns the reusable quantizer, continuous decoder and bounded binary format.
The report owns its visible fitting, file IO, evaluation and figures. The original three archives,
1,359,472 values, 110,972-byte bitstream, PNG collection and source identities are preserved.
The initial and selected prerequisite checkpoints reproduce 101,984 parameter values exactly.
Native Float64 MSE and global-PSNR reductions have small stated floating roundoff; elapsed
prerequisite time is measured anew. There are no codec optimizer updates or hidden transmitted
encoder parameters. This single fixed model and pool do not establish competitive compression,
semantic preservation or robustness on other images.

An optional independent NumPy/stdlib development check verifies 352,000 decoder/encoder
forward values, 6,496 exact symbols and dequantized coordinates, every packet byte, malformed
controls and all 200 lossless PNGs / 156,800 pixels. It does no preparation or training and is
separate from the complete native programs above.

> References

- Ballé, J., Laparra, V. and Simoncelli, E. P. (2017). [End-to-end Optimized Image Compression](https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01704).
  ICLR. The present frozen teaching autoencoder does not implement that paper's joint rate-distortion model.
- TFLCODE1 is the original local example's explicitly documented teaching format; it is not a general interchange standard.
